---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — Y.Org"
  description="Learn about Y.Org, the Y Window System: its architecture, security principles and approach to user control."
>
  <div class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Index</a>
    <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
    <span>About</span>
  </div>

  <header class="about-header">
    <h1>About Y.Org</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      Y.Org — The Y Window System — is a Linux graphical system
      project built around Rust, Wayland and user control, with
      a strong focus on security, transparency and application
      permissions.
    </p>
  </header>

  <article class="about-content">
    <section>
      <h2>What is Y.Org?</h2>

      <p>
        Y.Org is an open-source project developing an approach
        to graphical session management for Linux. It brings
        together window management, graphical applications and
        the components that make up a desktop session.
      </p>

      <p>
        Built around Wayland, Y.Org explores how a graphical
        environment can provide a modern foundation for desktop
        applications while keeping the user in control of
        access to sensitive resources.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>From X.Org to Y.Org</h2>

      <p>
        X.Org has played an important role in the history of
        graphical environments on Unix-like systems. Y.Org
        takes inspiration from this legacy while exploring a
        different approach to architecture, security and the
        relationship between applications and the desktop.
      </p>

      <p>
        Y.Org is built around Wayland rather than being a direct
        continuation of the X.Org server. Compatibility with
        existing applications, integration with other desktop
        components and migration paths are important
        considerations in its development.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why Y.Org?</h2>

      <p>
        Graphical applications rely on shared resources to
        provide a useful desktop experience. However, access
        to those resources should not automatically grant
        unrestricted control over the user's graphical session.
      </p>

      <p>
        Y.Org places application permissions and user
        authorization at the center of its security approach.
        Its design explores clearer boundaries around sensitive
        operations, helping users understand and manage what
        applications can access.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Design principles</h2>

      <div class="principle">
        <h3>User control</h3>
        <p>
          The user remains at the center of the graphical
          session, with meaningful control over sensitive
          operations and application access.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="principle">
        <h3>Security by design</h3>
        <p>
          Security is a core architectural consideration,
          shaping how components interact and how access to
          sensitive resources is handled.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="principle">
        <h3>Transparency</h3>
        <p>
          System behavior, permission mechanisms and technical
          decisions should be understandable and documented
          clearly.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="principle">
        <h3>Open development</h3>
        <p>
          Source code, development progress and design
          discussions are part of an open-source project that
          welcomes review and community participation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Technology</h2>

      <p>
        Y.Org is written in Rust and uses Wayland as the
        protocol for communication between the compositor and
        compatible clients. Rust provides the implementation
        language, while Vulkan serves as the planned graphics
        rendering technology.
      </p>

      <p>
        These technologies form the basis of Y.Org's technical
        direction. The architecture connects graphical session
        management with a design that prioritizes security,
        reliability, performance and user control.
      </p>

      <p>
        The implementation continues to evolve as the project
        develops. Specific capabilities depend on the
        components that are implemented and integrated.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Philosophy</h2>

      <p>
        The philosophy of Y.Org is simple: the computer belongs
        to its user, and the graphical environment should
        reflect that principle.
      </p>

      <p>
        Applications are an essential part of the desktop, but
        they should not automatically receive unrestricted
        authority over the session in which they run. Access
        to sensitive resources should respect clear security
        boundaries and the user's authority.
      </p>

      <p>
        Y.Org explores a model in which sensitive graphical
        operations are governed by explicit permissions and
        meaningful user authorization. Requests for access
        should be understandable, allowing users to make
        informed decisions about their own systems.
      </p>

      <p>
        Security should not come at the expense of user
        freedom. Instead, it should protect the user's ability
        to decide how their computer behaves while preventing
        applications from gaining unnecessary privileges.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ultimately, Y.Org puts the user at the center of the
        graphical session. The system defines and enforces
        security boundaries, while the user remains the
        authority those protections are designed to serve.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Project status</h2>

      <p>
        Y.Org is an evolving open-source project. Its
        architecture and implementation develop alongside its
        design principles, and the capabilities available in
        each release depend on the current state of development.
      </p>

      <p>
        Visit the <a href="/development/">Development</a> page
        to learn more about the project's technical direction,
        or explore the source code on
        <a
          href="https://github.com/The-Y-Org-Project/Y.Org"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-header {
    padding: 20px 0 25px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #c8ccd1;
    margin-bottom: 28px;
  }

  .about-header .lead {
    max-width: 790px;
    color: #54595d;
    font-family: "Gelasio", Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
    font-size: 19px;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .about-content {
    max-width: 850px;
  }

  .about-content section {
    margin-bottom: 30px;
  }

  .about-content h2 {
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  .about-content p {
    margin-bottom: 13px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .principle {
    padding: 12px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #eaecf0;
  }

  .principle:last-child {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  .principle h3 {
    margin: 0 0 5px;
    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  .principle p {
    margin: 0;
    color: #54595d;
  }

  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .about-header .lead {
      font-size: 17px;
    }
  }
</style>
